<?xml version="1.0"?>
<robot name="bucket" xmlns:xacro="http://ros.org/wiki/xacro">
  <!--
    Excavation bucket - a front-loader attachment that bolts to the front of
    the chassis.

    Source of truth: the Onshape export produced by
    ~/Documents/projects/onshape-urdf/to_urdf_test.py, kept for reference under
    perseus_description/cad/bucket/. Every number below is either measured off
    that export's meshes and mate transforms, or derived from them. The two
    exceptions are called out explicitly: material density and the mount pose.

    ── The mechanism ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    A symmetric double four-bar. Two 40x20 arms pivot on the upper pair of
    bearing blocks; two 250 mm actuators pivot on the lower pair 240 mm below
    and push brackets 597 mm out along the arms. A third 250 mm actuator runs
    from a plate on the arms to the bucket and tilts it.

    ── What is modelled, and why it differs ──────────────────────────────────
    URDF is a tree and cannot express the two closed loops. Re-closing them
    with raw SDF <joint> blocks (as cad/bucket/bucket_loops.urdf.xacro tries
    to) does not survive URDF->SDF conversion, because the links those joints
    reference hang off *fixed* joints and get welded into their parents first.

    So the linkage is reduced to the two independent degrees of freedom the
    loops leave behind:

        bucket_lift_joint  - arms pitch about the upper frame pivots
        bucket_tilt_joint  - bucket pitches about the arm tips

    Those names match the actuator groups the real robot drives over CAN -
    bucket_driver in the `payloads` package writes LIFT_BOTH and TILT_BOTH.

    The three rams ARE drawn, as barrel + sliding shaft, and they track the
    linkage exactly - see "Rams" below. They are cosmetic only: their mass
    stays lumped at their pin locations on the three load-bearing bodies, and
    they carry no collision geometry.

    ── The bucket is a clamshell ─────────────────────────────────────────────
    Bucket_1 is a subassembly: "Bucket FRONT Frame", "Bucket REAR Frame", a
    BucketClipVslotMount and two 50 mm actuators. Those actuators are the
    JAWS_BOTH group in bucket_driver. The export flattens all of it into one
    rigid body (cad.max_depth: 0) and so does this file - the jaws are a third
    DOF that is not modelled. The floor collision below spans the gap between
    the two halves, i.e. the jaws are simulated permanently closed.

    ── Rams ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Each ram is a barrel pinned to its parent by a revolute joint, plus a shaft
    on a prismatic joint. The two meshes come from splitting the CAD's fused
    250mm_Actuator_N.stl at its two disconnected solids, so they are the real
    parts, not stand-ins.

    Nothing in Gazebo can make them follow the linkage on their own:
      * a genuine closed loop is refused outright - gz-physics-dartsim answers
        "the child link already has a parent joint";
      * URDF <mimic> converts into SDF <mimic> cleanly, but dartsim in
        Gazebo Harmonic does not enforce it - the joint just sits frozen at
        zero (verified against a two-bar test model);
      * and the affine relation <mimic> could express is wrong by up to 30 mm
        at the rod end anyway, because beta(q) and s(q) are both nonlinear.

    So the ram angles and extensions are computed in closed form by
    perseus_simulation's bucket_ram_follower node and fed to the Gazebo
    position controllers below. That is exact, not an approximation. If the
    node is not running the rams simply hold their last pose - they have
    gravity disabled and negligible mass, so they neither droop nor perturb
    the linkage.
  -->

  <!-- ══ Density ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       The ONE genuine assumption in this file. Onshape reports
       "hasMass": false, "massMissingCount": 53 for this assembly - not a
       single part has a material assigned, which is why the export wrote
       mass=0 or mass=1 everywhere. The *volumes* are exact though (Onshape
       gives 4138.7 cm3 for the assembly; summing the meshes here gives
       4118.4 cm3, a 0.5% tessellation difference), so mass is volume x this.

       2700 kg/m3 is 6061 aluminium. The frame and rails measure 20x20 mm
       V-slot extrusion at ~70% fill, and the parts list contains a
       "BucketClipVslotMount", so extruded aluminium is the right call. For a
       steel fabrication use 7850 and every mass and inertia below scales by
       2.907 - they are all linear in density.

       To fix this properly: assign materials to every part in Onshape and
       re-export. Nothing else will. -->
  <xacro:property name="bucket_density" value="2700.0"/>

  <!-- ══ Where the attachment sits on the rover ═══════════════════════════
       The frame's two rearward rails run 243 mm back from the gantry at
       z = 0.018..0.038 in frame coordinates, 390 mm apart. They bolt to the
       UNDERSIDE of the chassis, so the rail top face sits flush with the
       chassis floor. The chassis link origin is the centre of its front face
       at the bottom, so that floor is chassis z = 0:

         z: 0 - (rail top 0.038 - arm pivot 0.213) = 0.175
         x: gantry front face 17 mm ahead of the chassis front face = 0.0591

       That puts the rails at chassis z -0.020..0.000 (base_link 0.330..0.350)
       and the gantry at base_link 0.255..0.555 - hanging 95 mm below the
       chassis floor, with 255 mm of ground clearance against a 150 mm wheel
       radius. It also drops the gantry top clear of the Livox at base_link
       0.700 by 145 mm, which an earlier deck-mounted guess did not. -->
  <xacro:property name="bucket_mount_x" value="0.0591"/>
  <xacro:property name="bucket_mount_y" value="0.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="bucket_mount_z" value="0.175"/>

  <!-- ══ Linkage geometry ════════════════════════════════════════════════
       All MEASURED, by forward-kinematicking the export's mate transforms
       into one frame. Mate transforms are rigid-body facts and are unaffected
       by the export's broken mass properties. -->
  <!-- arm pivot -> bucket pivot, along the arm -->
  <xacro:property name="arm_length" value="0.70004"/>
  <!-- lateral offset from the arm-pivot midpoint to the bucket pivot: the
       bucket hangs off ONE arm (Revolute_2 on Square_Tube_2), not both -->
  <xacro:property name="bucket_pivot_y" value="-0.10200"/>
  <!-- arm pivot -> lift-actuator bracket, along the arm (Fastened_2) -->
  <xacro:property name="lift_rod_offset" value="0.59712"/>
  <!-- arm pivot above the lift-actuator pivot -->
  <xacro:property name="lift_pivot_drop" value="0.24000"/>
  <!-- the lift bracket sits this far below the pivot line, hence the phase -->
  <xacro:property name="lift_phase" value="-0.016763"/>   <!-- -0.96 deg -->
  <!-- pin-to-pin length of a "250mm Actuator" as drawn in CAD = retracted -->
  <xacro:property name="actuator_retracted" value="0.36500"/>
  <xacro:property name="actuator_stroke" value="0.25000"/>

  <!-- ══ Lift travel ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       DERIVED from the above. With r the bracket radius, d the pivot drop and
       a the arm angle measured from the CAD pose,

           L(a)^2 = r^2 + d^2 + 2*r*d*sin(a + lift_phase)

       Retracted (L = 0.365) gives a = -77.61 deg; extended (L = 0.615) gives
       a = -6.24 deg. So the arms swing 71.36 deg, and at full extension they
       sit 6.24 deg below the frame's own x axis - they never quite reach
       horizontal. Joint zero is placed at full extension (top of travel,
       transport pose) by baking that 6.24 deg into the joint origin.

       Worth knowing: the CAD's *exported* pose needs L = 0.640, which is 25 mm
       beyond full extension. That is not an error in the CAD - the toolkit
       places each link by its own mate transform and never solves the loop, so
       the exported pose is the mate zero, not an assembly configuration. It is
       also why the pose in cad/bucket/ has the actuators visibly detached from
       their brackets. -->
  <xacro:property name="lift_len_max"
                  value="${sqrt(lift_rod_offset*lift_rod_offset + lift_pivot_drop*lift_pivot_drop)}"/>
  <xacro:property name="lift_sin_ext"
                  value="${((actuator_retracted + actuator_stroke)**2
                            - lift_rod_offset*lift_rod_offset - lift_pivot_drop*lift_pivot_drop)
                           / (2*lift_rod_offset*lift_pivot_drop)}"/>
  <xacro:property name="lift_sin_ret"
                  value="${(actuator_retracted**2
                            - lift_rod_offset*lift_rod_offset - lift_pivot_drop*lift_pivot_drop)
                           / (2*lift_rod_offset*lift_pivot_drop)}"/>
  <!-- nose-down pitch of the arm at joint zero, relative to the frame -->
  <xacro:property name="lift_zero_pitch" value="${-(asin(lift_sin_ext) - lift_phase)}"/>
  <xacro:property name="lift_lower" value="${asin(lift_sin_ret) - asin(lift_sin_ext)}"/>
  <xacro:property name="lift_upper" value="0.0"/>

  <!-- ══ Tilt travel ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Same treatment. The tilt actuator works between a pin on the bucket
       131.1 mm from the bucket pivot and a bracket on the arm 513.5 mm from
       it. The included angle is 108.25 deg in the CAD pose. Extending to
       0.615 m opens it to 136.34 deg (the bucket dumps); retracting closes it,
       but the actuator's 0.365 m retracted length is unreachable - the two
       pins go colinear at 0.382 m first, so the geometric stop is the fold at
       0 deg, i.e. 1.889 rad of curl.

       Curl is capped short of that fold: past about 1.6 rad the bucket is
       folded back over the arms, which the real structure will not allow.
       Dump is the true actuator limit. -->
  <xacro:property name="tilt_lower" value="-0.490398"/>
  <xacro:property name="tilt_upper" value="1.600000"/>
  <xacro:property name="tilt_fold_limit" value="1.889229"/>  <!-- geometric, for reference -->

  <!-- ══ Actuator force and speed ════════════════════════════════════════
       0.1 m/s is ACTUATOR_MAX_SPEED from bucket_driver.cpp in the `payloads`
       package - the real robot's own number. Force is a 1.5 kN class 250 mm
       actuator through the measured moment arms: 224 mm at the lift pivot,
       113 mm at the tilt pivot. For scale, holding the arm and a full bucket
       out straight takes about 41 Nm. -->
  <xacro:property name="lift_moment_arm" value="0.2239"/>
  <xacro:property name="tilt_moment_arm" value="0.1126"/>
  <xacro:property name="actuator_force" value="1500.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="actuator_speed" value="0.1"/>
  <xacro:property name="lift_effort" value="${actuator_force*lift_moment_arm}"/>
  <xacro:property name="tilt_effort" value="${actuator_force*tilt_moment_arm}"/>
  <xacro:property name="lift_velocity" value="${actuator_speed/lift_moment_arm}"/>
  <xacro:property name="tilt_velocity" value="${actuator_speed/tilt_moment_arm}"/>

  <!-- ══ Masses: exact CAD volume x density ══════════════════════════════ -->
  <xacro:property name="v_frame" value="0.00051813"/>   <!-- Frame_Repaired_1     -->
  <xacro:property name="v_plate" value="0.00015904"/>   <!-- one bearing block    -->
  <xacro:property name="v_tube" value="0.00016847"/>    <!-- one arm, 40x20x2 tube-->
  <xacro:property name="v_bracket" value="0.00001293"/> <!-- one actuator bracket -->
  <xacro:property name="v_arm_mount" value="0.00003805"/><!-- one on-arm plate    -->
  <xacro:property name="v_actuator" value="0.00040493"/><!-- one 250 mm actuator  -->
  <xacro:property name="v_bucket" value="0.00129748"/>  <!-- the whole clamshell  -->

  <xacro:property name="m_frame" value="${v_frame*bucket_density}"/>
  <xacro:property name="m_plate" value="${v_plate*bucket_density}"/>
  <xacro:property name="m_tube" value="${v_tube*bucket_density}"/>
  <xacro:property name="m_bracket" value="${v_bracket*bucket_density}"/>
  <xacro:property name="m_arm_mount" value="${v_arm_mount*bucket_density}"/>
  <xacro:property name="m_half_act" value="${v_actuator*bucket_density/2}"/>
  <xacro:property name="m_bucket" value="${v_bucket*bucket_density}"/>

  <!-- Body totals and their inertia tensors were composed from the meshes
       (exact tetrahedron integration, then parallel axis) plus the lumped
       actuator halves. At 2700 kg/m3 they come to 4.210 / 2.860 / 4.050 kg.
       Both mass and inertia are linear in density, so the scale factor below
       keeps the whole file correct if you change bucket_density. -->
  <xacro:property name="k" value="${bucket_density/2700.0}"/>

  <material name="bucket_extrusion">
    <color rgba="0.66 0.68 0.70 1.0"/>
  </material>
  <material name="bucket_arm_blue">
    <color rgba="0.13 0.25 0.50 1.0"/>
  </material>
  <material name="bucket_dark">
    <color rgba="0.20 0.22 0.25 1.0"/>
  </material>
  <material name="bucket_steel">
    <color rgba="0.55 0.60 0.65 1.0"/>
  </material>

  <xacro:property name="M" value="file://$(find perseus_description)/meshes/bucket"/>

  <!-- ══ Rams ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       One ram = barrel (revolute on its parent) + shaft (prismatic on the
       barrel). `phi` rotates the joint zero so the travel range does not
       straddle +/-pi; the follower node publishes beta + phi.

       Mass is a token 20 g: the real 1.09 kg per ram is already lumped at the
       pin locations on the frame, arm and bucket, and double-counting it here
       would change the linkage dynamics. No collision geometry - nothing is
       meant to hit a ram, and leaving it out keeps contact cheap.
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <xacro:macro name="bucket_ram" params="name parent mesh xyz mesh_rpy axis phi mesh_xyz:='0 0 0'">
    <link name="${prefix}${name}_barrel">
      <inertial>
        <origin xyz="0.15 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <mass value="0.02"/>
        <xacro:box_inertia m="0.02" x="0.34" y="0.06" z="0.06"/>
      </inertial>
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="${mesh_xyz}" rpy="${mesh_rpy}"/>
        <geometry><mesh filename="${M}/${mesh}_base.stl"/></geometry>
        <material name="bucket_steel"/>
      </visual>
    </link>

    <joint name="${prefix}${name}_pitch_joint" type="revolute">
      <parent link="${prefix}${parent}"/>
      <child link="${prefix}${name}_barrel"/>
      <origin xyz="${xyz}" rpy="0 ${phi} 0"/>
      <axis xyz="0 -1 0"/>
      <limit lower="-1.8" upper="1.8" effort="50" velocity="5"/>
      <dynamics damping="0.5"/>
    </joint>

    <link name="${prefix}${name}_shaft">
      <inertial>
        <origin xyz="0.25 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <mass value="0.02"/>
        <xacro:box_inertia m="0.02" x="0.29" y="0.02" z="0.02"/>
      </inertial>
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="${mesh_xyz}" rpy="${mesh_rpy}"/>
        <geometry><mesh filename="${M}/${mesh}_slider.stl"/></geometry>
        <material name="bucket_dark"/>
      </visual>
    </link>

    <joint name="${prefix}${name}_extend_joint" type="prismatic">
      <parent link="${prefix}${name}_barrel"/>
      <child link="${prefix}${name}_shaft"/>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <axis xyz="${axis}"/>
      <limit lower="0" upper="${actuator_stroke}" effort="50" velocity="0.5"/>
      <dynamics damping="0.5"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- Cosmetic: no gravity, so they hold their pose if the follower node is
         not running rather than collapsing. -->
    <gazebo reference="${prefix}${name}_barrel"><gravity>false</gravity></gazebo>
    <gazebo reference="${prefix}${name}_shaft"><gravity>false</gravity></gazebo>
  </xacro:macro>

  <!--
    parent  - link to bolt the frame to; the chassis front face by default.
    prefix  - inherited from the caller, as rocker and sensor_mount do.
    mount_* - override to re-site the whole attachment; nothing else changes.
  -->
  <xacro:macro name="bucket" params="
      parent:='${prefix}chassis'
      prefix:=^|''
      mount_xyz:='${bucket_mount_x} ${bucket_mount_y} ${bucket_mount_z}'
      mount_rpy:='0 0 0'">

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Interface link, at the midpoint of the two arm pivots with base_link
         axes: +x forward, +y left, +z up. Everything downstream hangs off it,
         so the rest of the robot only ever needs this one name.
         ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <link name="${prefix}bucket_mounting_frame"/>

    <joint name="${prefix}bucket_mount_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent link="${parent}"/>
      <child link="${prefix}bucket_mounting_frame"/>
      <origin xyz="${mount_xyz}" rpy="${mount_rpy}"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Frame: a 410 x 300 mm gantry of 20x20 extrusion carrying the four
         bearing blocks, with two rails running 243 mm back onto the chassis
         deck. Mesh origins are the export's own transforms, re-expressed
         about the arm-pivot midpoint.
         ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <link name="${prefix}bucket_frame">
      <inertial>
        <origin xyz="-0.033899 0.019998 -0.155044" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <mass value="${4.2099*k}"/>
        <inertia ixx="${0.114994*k}" ixy="${0.0*k}"      ixz="${0.001957*k}"
                                     iyy="${0.060850*k}" iyz="${0.0*k}"
                                                         izz="${0.072903*k}"/>
      </inertial>

      <visual>
        <origin xyz="-0.05910 -0.44071 -0.21300" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <geometry><mesh filename="${M}/Frame_Repaired_1.stl"/></geometry>
        <material name="bucket_extrusion"/>
      </visual>
      <!-- four bearing blocks: upper pair carries the arms, lower pair the
           lift actuators -->
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="-0.04169  0.16600 -0.21995" rpy="1.5707963 0 -1.5707963"/>
        <geometry><mesh filename="${M}/Main_Bucket_Mount_Plate_v8_1.stl"/></geometry>
        <material name="bucket_dark"/>
      </visual>
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="-0.04169 -0.03400 -0.21995" rpy="1.5707963 0 -1.5707963"/>
        <geometry><mesh filename="${M}/Main_Bucket_Mount_Plate_v8_2.stl"/></geometry>
        <material name="bucket_dark"/>
      </visual>
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="-0.04169 -0.03400  0.02005" rpy="1.5707963 0 -1.5707963"/>
        <geometry><mesh filename="${M}/Main_Bucket_Mount_Plate_v8_3.stl"/></geometry>
        <material name="bucket_dark"/>
      </visual>
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="-0.04169  0.16600  0.02005" rpy="1.5707963 0 -1.5707963"/>
        <geometry><mesh filename="${M}/Main_Bucket_Mount_Plate_v8_4.stl"/></geometry>
        <material name="bucket_dark"/>
      </visual>

      <!-- Boxes, not the mesh. This link welds into the chassis during SDF
           conversion anyway, so a 672 kB concave mesh collision would cost
           contact time for nothing. Extents are the measured mesh bounds. -->
      <collision name="${prefix}bucket_gantry_collision">
        <origin xyz="-0.05040 0.02000 -0.12000" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <geometry><box size="0.020 0.410 0.300"/></geometry>
      </collision>
      <collision name="${prefix}bucket_rail_left_collision">
        <origin xyz="-0.18060 0.20500 -0.18500" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <geometry><box size="0.243 0.020 0.020"/></geometry>
      </collision>
      <collision name="${prefix}bucket_rail_right_collision">
        <origin xyz="-0.18060 -0.16500 -0.18500" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <geometry><box size="0.243 0.020 0.020"/></geometry>
      </collision>
    </link>

    <joint name="${prefix}bucket_frame_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent link="${prefix}bucket_mounting_frame"/>
      <child link="${prefix}bucket_frame"/>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Arms: both tubes, both lift brackets, the tilt-actuator plate and its
         bracket, as one rigid body - the bucket pins them together at the far
         end so they cannot move relative to each other.

         Both tubes are the same part ("Left Arm - Square Tube" <1> and <2>);
         there is no right-hand mirror in the CAD, so both are offset the same
         way from their pivots rather than symmetrically. That is reproduced
         faithfully here.
         ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <link name="${prefix}bucket_arm">
      <inertial>
        <origin xyz="0.407179 0.019695 -0.000497" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <mass value="${2.8599*k}"/>
        <inertia ixx="${0.022280*k}" ixy="${0.000424*k}" ixz="${0.004437*k}"
                                     iyy="${0.125578*k}" iyz="${0.000055*k}"
                                                         izz="${0.147197*k}"/>
      </inertial>

      <visual>
        <origin xyz="0.00004  0.10000 -0.00001" rpy="1.5707963 0 0"/>
        <geometry><mesh filename="${M}/Left_Arm_-_Square_Tube_1.stl"/></geometry>
        <material name="bucket_arm_blue"/>
      </visual>
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="0.00004 -0.10000 -0.00001" rpy="1.5707963 0 0"/>
        <geometry><mesh filename="${M}/Left_Arm_-_Square_Tube_2.stl"/></geometry>
        <material name="bucket_arm_blue"/>
      </visual>
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="0.59704 -0.08000 -0.01001" rpy="0 0 -1.5707963"/>
        <geometry><mesh filename="${M}/Actuator_Bracket_1.stl"/></geometry>
        <material name="bucket_dark"/>
      </visual>
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="0.59704  0.12000 -0.01001" rpy="0 0 -1.5707963"/>
        <geometry><mesh filename="${M}/Actuator_Bracket_2.stl"/></geometry>
        <material name="bucket_dark"/>
      </visual>
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="0.22674 -0.08000  0.00999" rpy="3.1415927 0 0"/>
        <geometry><mesh filename="${M}/Acutator_on_Arm_Mount_1.stl"/></geometry>
        <material name="bucket_steel"/>
      </visual>
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="0.17904 -0.08000  0.01199" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <geometry><mesh filename="${M}/Acutator_on_Arm_Mount_3.stl"/></geometry>
        <material name="bucket_steel"/>
      </visual>
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="0.18674  0.02000  0.01399" rpy="-3.1415927 0 -1.5707963"/>
        <geometry><mesh filename="${M}/Actuator_Bracket_4.stl"/></geometry>
        <material name="bucket_dark"/>
      </visual>

      <!-- one box per tube, sized from the measured 750 x 40 x 20 extents -->
      <collision name="${prefix}bucket_arm_left_collision">
        <origin xyz="0.35200 0.12000 0.00000" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <geometry><box size="0.750 0.040 0.020"/></geometry>
      </collision>
      <collision name="${prefix}bucket_arm_right_collision">
        <origin xyz="0.35200 -0.08000 0.00000" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <geometry><box size="0.750 0.040 0.020"/></geometry>
      </collision>
    </link>

    <!-- Positive raises. Zero is the top of travel, with the arms 6.24 deg
         below the frame axis because that is as far as the actuators reach. -->
    <joint name="${prefix}bucket_lift_joint" type="revolute">
      <parent link="${prefix}bucket_frame"/>
      <child link="${prefix}bucket_arm"/>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 ${lift_zero_pitch} 0"/>
      <axis xyz="0 -1 0"/>
      <limit lower="${lift_lower}" upper="${lift_upper}"
             effort="${lift_effort}" velocity="${lift_velocity}"/>
      <!-- These are self-locking screw actuators, so the linkage is heavily
           damped rather than free-swinging. It also keeps the position PID
           from ringing. -->
      <dynamics damping="20.0" friction="5.0"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Bucket. Link origin is the tilt pivot; the scoop sits forward of and
         above it. Measured envelope, in this frame: x -0.046..0.210 deep,
         y -0.108..0.352 wide (459 mm), z -0.044..0.192 tall.
         ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <link name="${prefix}bucket">
      <inertial>
        <origin xyz="0.035308 0.120650 0.040549" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <mass value="${4.0499*k}"/>
        <inertia ixx="${0.110028*k}" ixy="${0.000050*k}" ixz="${0.000734*k}"
                                     iyy="${0.023912*k}" iyz="${0.000452*k}"
                                                         izz="${0.103688*k}"/>
      </inertial>

      <visual>
        <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 -1.5707963 -1.5707963"/>
        <geometry><mesh filename="${M}/Bucket_1.stl"/></geometry>
        <material name="bucket_steel"/>
      </visual>

      <!-- Four boxes fitted to the measured plate positions: floor, rear wall
           and the two side structures. Keeping them separate is what makes the
           scoop concave to the physics engine - one bounding box would be a
           brick, and the 637 kB concave mesh is neither fast nor stable as a
           collision shape. The floor spans the gap between the clamshell
           halves, i.e. the jaws are simulated closed. -->
      <collision name="${prefix}bucket_floor_collision">
        <origin xyz="0.12500 0.12200 -0.03900" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <geometry><box size="0.170 0.460 0.010"/></geometry>
      </collision>
      <collision name="${prefix}bucket_rear_collision">
        <origin xyz="0.04500 0.12200 0.03300" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <geometry><box size="0.010 0.460 0.145"/></geometry>
      </collision>
      <collision name="${prefix}bucket_side_left_collision">
        <origin xyz="0.12500 0.32700 0.03600" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <geometry><box size="0.170 0.049 0.150"/></geometry>
      </collision>
      <collision name="${prefix}bucket_side_right_collision">
        <origin xyz="0.12500 -0.09600 0.03600" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <geometry><box size="0.170 0.023 0.150"/></geometry>
      </collision>
    </link>

    <!-- Positive curls the scoop back to retain a load; negative dumps.
         Zero is the CAD pose. -->
    <joint name="${prefix}bucket_tilt_joint" type="revolute">
      <parent link="${prefix}bucket_arm"/>
      <child link="${prefix}bucket"/>
      <origin xyz="${arm_length} ${bucket_pivot_y} -0.00001" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <axis xyz="0 -1 0"/>
      <limit lower="${tilt_lower}" upper="${tilt_upper}"
             effort="${tilt_effort}" velocity="${tilt_velocity}"/>
      <dynamics damping="10.0" friction="2.0"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         The three 250 mm rams. Pivots and mesh orientations are the export's
         own transforms; both lift rams share one angle and one extension
         because their pivots and brackets differ only laterally.
         ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <!-- The pivot y values are the CAD ones shifted by ~10 mm so that each rod
         end lands exactly on its bracket. In the real machine the ram pivots
         and the brackets they drive are ~10 mm apart laterally and the rod-end
         bearings take up the skew; a revolute joint cannot, so the skew is
         moved to the base pin instead, where it is buried inside the bearing
         block rather than showing as a gap at the visible rod end. Verified
         over the whole workspace: rod end lands within 0.01 mm. -->
    <xacro:bucket_ram name="ram_lift_left"  parent="bucket_frame" mesh="250mm_Actuator_1"
                      xyz="0.00004 0.119074 -0.24001"
                      mesh_rpy="0 -1.5707963 -1.5707963" axis="1 0 0" phi="0"/>
    <xacro:bucket_ram name="ram_lift_right" parent="bucket_frame" mesh="250mm_Actuator_2"
                      xyz="0.00004 -0.079074 -0.24001"
                      mesh_rpy="0 -1.5707963 1.5707963" axis="1 0 0" phi="0"/>
    <!-- The tilt ram's barrel is bolted to the plate on the ARMS (the
         Acutator_on_Arm_Mount pair, via Actuator_Bracket_4) and its rod reaches
         forward to the bucket. The CAD export describes it the other way round
         - Revolute_9's parent is Bucket_1 - because the toolkit roots each mate
         at whichever body it walked to first; anchoring it to the arm is what
         the machine actually does, and it is what makes the barrel stay put
         while the rod telescopes.

         mesh_xyz places the actuator mesh so its arm-side pin sits on the joint
         rather than its origin. -->
    <xacro:bucket_ram name="ram_tilt" parent="bucket_arm" mesh="250mm_Actuator_3"
                      xyz="0.18674 0.030248 0.01399"
                      mesh_xyz="0.365 -0.02025 0"
                      mesh_rpy="0 1.5707963 -1.5707963" axis="1 0 0" phi="0"/>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Gazebo
         ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <!-- The cutting edge needs grip to bite rather than skate. -->
    <gazebo reference="${prefix}bucket">
      <mu1>1.0</mu1>
      <mu2>1.0</mu2>
    </gazebo>
    <gazebo reference="${prefix}bucket_arm">
      <mu1>0.6</mu1>
      <mu2>0.6</mu2>
    </gazebo>

    <!--
      The bucket joints are deliberately NOT in the <ros2_control> block. On
      the real robot they are not ros2_control joints either - bucket_driver in
      the `payloads` package drives them straight over CAN. Gazebo's own
      position controllers give the same shape of interface without pulling in
      ros2_controllers packages that are absent from the pixi environment
      (only diff_drive, mecanum_drive and joint_state_broadcaster are there).

      Command them, in radians, absolute:
        ros2 topic pub /bucket/lift/cmd_pos std_msgs/msg/Float64 "{data: -0.6}"
        ros2 topic pub /bucket/tilt/cmd_pos std_msgs/msg/Float64 "{data: 0.8}"
      Both are bridged in perseus_simulation/config/gz_bridge.yaml.

      Gains hold a loaded arm: worst-case static torque at the lift pivot is
      about 41 Nm, so p_gain has to make that from a small error. cmd_max is
      the joint's own effort limit.
    -->
    <gazebo>
      <plugin filename="gz-sim-joint-position-controller-system"
              name="gz::sim::systems::JointPositionController">
        <joint_name>${prefix}bucket_lift_joint</joint_name>
        <topic>/bucket/lift/cmd_pos</topic>
        <!--
          Sized from the mechanics rather than by twiddling. The arm plus
          bucket present about 2.8 kg.m2 at this pivot and need up to 41 Nm to
          hold out straight, so p has to make that torque from an error small
          enough to be invisible: 8000 does it in 0.005 rad. d is then set for
          roughly critical damping at the resulting 53 rad/s, and i only has to
          mop up what is left - bounded well below the gravity load so a large
          step cannot wind it up into an overshoot.
        -->
        <p_gain>8000</p_gain>
        <i_gain>300</i_gain>
        <d_gain>400</d_gain>
        <i_max>80</i_max>
        <i_min>-80</i_min>
        <cmd_max>${lift_effort}</cmd_max>
        <cmd_min>${-lift_effort}</cmd_min>
        <initial_position>0.0</initial_position>
      </plugin>

      <plugin filename="gz-sim-joint-position-controller-system"
              name="gz::sim::systems::JointPositionController">
        <joint_name>${prefix}bucket_tilt_joint</joint_name>
        <topic>/bucket/tilt/cmd_pos</topic>
        <!-- The bucket alone is only ~0.036 kg.m2 about the tilt pin and
             needs ~2 Nm to hold, so this joint wants far softer gains than the
             lift. Pushing p much past 1000 puts the natural frequency close to
             what a 1 ms step can integrate. -->
        <p_gain>1000</p_gain>
        <i_gain>400</i_gain>
        <d_gain>100</d_gain>
        <i_max>40</i_max>
        <i_min>-40</i_min>
        <cmd_max>${tilt_effort}</cmd_max>
        <cmd_min>${-tilt_effort}</cmd_min>
        <initial_position>0.0</initial_position>
      </plugin>

      <!--
        joint_state_broadcaster only reports the four wheel joints, because
        only those are in <ros2_control>. Without this the bucket joints never
        reach robot_state_publisher and the attachment sits frozen at zero in
        RViz however it moves in Gazebo.

        The topic matches the `joint_state` entry already in gz_bridge.yaml,
        and only the two bucket joints are listed so nothing fights
        joint_state_broadcaster over the wheels.
      -->
      <plugin filename="gz-sim-joint-state-publisher-system"
              name="gz::sim::systems::JointStatePublisher">
        <topic>joint_state</topic>
        <joint_name>${prefix}bucket_lift_joint</joint_name>
        <joint_name>${prefix}bucket_tilt_joint</joint_name>
        <joint_name>${prefix}ram_lift_left_pitch_joint</joint_name>
        <joint_name>${prefix}ram_lift_left_extend_joint</joint_name>
        <joint_name>${prefix}ram_lift_right_pitch_joint</joint_name>
        <joint_name>${prefix}ram_lift_right_extend_joint</joint_name>
        <joint_name>${prefix}ram_tilt_pitch_joint</joint_name>
        <joint_name>${prefix}ram_tilt_extend_joint</joint_name>
      </plugin>

      <!--
        Ram followers. Both lift rams subscribe to the SAME pair of topics -
        their geometry is identical bar a lateral offset - so the follower node
        only has to publish four numbers.

        These links are near-massless with gravity off, so the gains only have
        to overcome joint damping. Keeping them low avoids adding a stiff,
        pointless mode to the solver.
      -->
      <xacro:macro name="ram_follower" params="joint topic kind">
        <plugin filename="gz-sim-joint-position-controller-system"
                name="gz::sim::systems::JointPositionController">
          <joint_name>${prefix}${joint}</joint_name>
          <topic>${topic}</topic>
          <xacro:if value="${kind == 'pitch'}">
            <p_gain>20</p_gain><i_gain>2</i_gain><d_gain>2</d_gain>
          </xacro:if>
          <xacro:unless value="${kind == 'pitch'}">
            <p_gain>200</p_gain><i_gain>20</i_gain><d_gain>10</d_gain>
          </xacro:unless>
          <i_max>5</i_max>
          <i_min>-5</i_min>
          <cmd_max>50</cmd_max>
          <cmd_min>-50</cmd_min>
          <initial_position>0.0</initial_position>
        </plugin>
      </xacro:macro>

      <xacro:ram_follower joint="ram_lift_left_pitch_joint"   topic="/bucket/ram_lift/cmd_angle"  kind="pitch"/>
      <xacro:ram_follower joint="ram_lift_right_pitch_joint"  topic="/bucket/ram_lift/cmd_angle"  kind="pitch"/>
      <xacro:ram_follower joint="ram_lift_left_extend_joint"  topic="/bucket/ram_lift/cmd_extend" kind="extend"/>
      <xacro:ram_follower joint="ram_lift_right_extend_joint" topic="/bucket/ram_lift/cmd_extend" kind="extend"/>
      <xacro:ram_follower joint="ram_tilt_pitch_joint"        topic="/bucket/ram_tilt/cmd_angle"  kind="pitch"/>
      <xacro:ram_follower joint="ram_tilt_extend_joint"       topic="/bucket/ram_tilt/cmd_extend" kind="extend"/>
    </gazebo>

    <!--
      Sensor clearance: the gantry occupies base_link x 0.600..0.617,
      z 0.255..0.555. The Livox at (0.600, 0, 0.700) and the D455 at
      (0.600, 0, 0.600) both clear its top. The bucket itself is still in the
      LiDAR's forward-down view when the arms are raised, which is true of the
      real machine as well.
    -->

  </xacro:macro>
</robot>
